    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d235">

                <pb facs="0340" n="324" sameAs="frus:frus1948v09/pg324"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.00/7–348: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in Belgium </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-07-03T12:00:00-04:00">July 3,
                            1948—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1036. Frequent reports of continued activities of Van Loo (US Mil Attaché Airgram
                    Nr. AGC–97, 25 June 1948<anchor n="1" xml:id="d235-f1"/>) and like-minded
                    individuals becoming source of increasing irritation. While we understand Belg
                    business instincts we do not understand seeming indifference of Belg Govt to
                    activities having effect of syphoning off mil potential of Brussels Pact powers.
                    We should think that Spaak would have even stronger feeling in this respect
                    since ultimate action on part of US in implementing Vandenberg Resolution<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d235-f2"/> depends upon preliminary action by Brussels
                    signatories.</p>
                <p>We would consider that placing by Belg Representatives of arms <pb facs="0341"
                        n="325" xml:id="pg325"/> exports question before Five Power Mil Commission
                    would be definite step forward.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d235-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Brussels as 1036 rptd Lond as 2549 Paris as 2463 and Hague as 302.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d235-f1" n="1" xml:id="d235-f1-content">
                <p>Not found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d235-f2" n="2" xml:id="d235-f2-content">
                <p>For the text of Senator Vandenberg’s Resolution of June 11, 1948, see the first
                    compilation in <ref target="frus1948v03">volume <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >iii</hi>
                    </ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d235-f3" n="3" xml:id="d235-f3-content">
                <p>In telegram 1387, July 7, Embassy Brussels reported that the Belgian Government
                    had referred the question to the Five Power Military Commission (501.BB
                    Palestine/7–748).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
